n award-winning developer of drone solutions, software, and robotics, today announced the...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLa présidente du Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec souligne le décès de monsieur Michel Gauthier (https://www.assnat.qc.ca/fra/cepanq/fra/communiques/comm228.htm)\nciteturn16search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.2 years ago; Content type: text/plain; \nLa présidente du Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec souligne le décès de monsieur Michel Gauthier La présidente du Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec souligne le décès de monsieur Michel Gauthier Québec, le 2 juin 2020 – La présidente du Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec, madame Rita Dionne-Marsolais, a appris avec tristesse le décès d’un ancien député de l’Assemblée nationale, monsieur Michel Gauthier, survenu le 30 mai dernier. Titulaire d'un brevet en enseignement de l'École normale de Roberval en 1969, d'un baccalauréat en enseignement élémentaire de l'Université du Québec à Chicoutimi en 1975, d'un certificat en gestion des ressources humaines de la Télé-université en 1990 et d'un diplôme en administration scolaire de l'Université de Sherbrooke en 1993, Michel Gauthier a travaillé plusieurs années à la Commission scolaire de Roberval. Il a été enseignant de 1970 à 1975, conseiller pédagogique de 1976 à 1979, directeur des services de l'enseignement de 1979 à 1981, et directeur général de 1988 à 1993. Élu député du Parti québécois dans Roberval en 1981, monsieur Gauthier a siégé à l’Assemblée nationale jusqu’au 2 février 1988, date de sa démission. Pendant cette période, il fut notamment adjoint parlementaire au ministre des Finances du 23 février 1983 au 23 octobre 1985 et vice-président de la Commission des affaires sociales du 11 février 1986 au 2 février 1988. Michel Gauthier a été élu député du Bloc québécois dans Roberval à l'élection fédérale de 1993. Réélu en 1997, en 2000, en 2004 et, dans Roberval–Lac-Saint-Jean, en 2006, monsieur Gauthier a siégé à la Chambre des communes jusqu’au 29 juillet 2007. Il a occupé les postes de leader parlementaire de l'opposition officielle de 1993 à 1996, chef du Bloc québécois et chef de l'opposition officielle du 17 février 1996 au 15 mars 1997, et leader parlementaire du Bloc québécois du 4 juin 1997 au 5 juillet 2007. Monsieur Gauthier fut président de la Corporation touristique de Chambord en 1975 et en 1976, président de la Chambre de commerce de Chambord en 1977 et en 1978, et animateur de l'émission d'affaires publiques Gauthier au réseau TQS d'avril 2007 à mai 2008. Il fut décoré du grade d'officier de l'Ordre de la Pléiade en 1999. Le Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec tient à offrir ses plus vives condoléances à la conjointe et aux membres de la famille et aux proches de monsieur Michel Gauthier. Source et renseignements : Manon Voyer Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l’Assemblée nationale du Québec Manon.Voyer@assnat.qc.ca © Assemblée nationale du Québec Le Cercle des ex-parlementaires de l'Assemblée nationale du Québec a pour mission première de regrouper les femmes et les hommes qui ont siégé ensemble ou dans des législatures différentes.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLGen Michel Gauthier at the Special Committee on the Canadian Mission in Afghanistan | openparliament.ca (https://openparliament.ca/committees/afghanistan/40-2/16/lgen-michel-gauthier-1/only/)\nciteturn16search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3.3 years ago; Content type: text/plain; \nLGen Michel Gauthier at the Special Committee on the Canadian Mission in Afghanistan | openparliament.ca Home Committees Afghanistan 2009 Nov. 25 LGen Michel Gauthier LGen Michel Gauthier at the Special Committee on the Canadian Mission in Afghanistan On November 25th, 2009. See this statement in context. Permalink November 25th, 2009 / 3:50 p.m. LGen Michel Gauthier Thank you. I will make my statement in English but I am obviously ready to answer any questions in French. Let me start with some context. On February 1, 2006, I was given command of Canadian Expeditionary Force Command, based here in Ottawa, as its very first commander, and I became responsible to the CDS for all Canadian military operations overseas, including, of course, the mission in Afghanistan. I held these responsibilities for almost three and a half years. Over that timeframe, more than 24,000 men and women served under my command on 28 different missions around the globe. I had a staff in my headquarters back here in Ottawa of more than 200 very capable civilian and military people. Many of these folks were Afghanistan veterans, and all, I can assure you, were completely devoted to listening to and supporting our people in the field, whether they were from the Canadian Forces, Foreign Affairs, CIDA, or wherever else. I have just retired after 36 years of serving my country to what I consider to be the best of my ability, much of that in quite demanding appointments. I consider it an incredible honour to have been trusted with responsibility for some of the CF's most important, difficult, and dangerous endeavours--not just in Afghanistan, as you heard from General Hillier, but also at sea, with some very dynamic maritime security and counter-piracy missions that presented their own unique set of challenges. I was reminded pretty starkly of the seriousness of my responsibilities, and of just what's at stake in Afghanistan, each and every time my phone rang in the middle of the night, as it did way too often, with Dave Fraser or others on the line with the sad details of another tough incident. Equally sobering and humbling for me, but always inspiring, were the many trips to Trenton with General Hillier or General Natynczyk to meet personally with the families of the fallen in the darkest ... statement of fact for the first time on national television last week. Thereafter, between April 20, 2007, and the first alert on June 4, 2007, many of the reports received do in fact deal with issues of torture. This was at a time when DFAIT and the rest of us were completely engaged in reviewing our posture, and the reports were provided in a dynamic process between DFAIT in particular and the field. Given what's at stake here, you can rest assured that l've read all of these many times, both the full classified versions and the recently redacted versions. There is, in my view, little room for interpretation. There was nothing in the reports that caused me or my staff to see in them serious, imminent, or alarming new warnings of torture before the June 2007 reports, and to suggest that senior military officials or commanders ignored these or covered them up is wrong. The second point I want to address very briefly is the comment in last week's testimony that senior officials in DFAIT and the Canadian Forces did not welcome reports or advice from the field and that at first they were mostly ignored. This criticism ... branding us war criminals on national television. That damage to my reputation is done and I have to accept that as a consequence of being in a...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDraganfly Inc. Appoints Lieutenant-General (Ret'd) Michel Gauthier to Military Advisory Board, Effective February 20, 2026 | MarketScreener (https://www.marketscreener.com/news/draganfly-inc-appoints-lieutenant-general-ret-d-michel-gauthier-to-military-advisory-board-effec-ce7e5dddd188f024)\nciteturn16search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Content type: text/plain; \nDraganfly Inc. Appoints Lieutenant-General (Ret'd) Michel Gauthier to Military Advisory Board, Effective February 20, 2026 | MarketScreener DRAGANFLY INC. Add to a list PDF Report Draganfly Inc. Stocks DPRO CA26142Q3044 Aerospace & Defense Delayed Canadian Securities 